#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "870077e3-99f9-52b9-ac39-26bbc3827466" :question/math-academy-id "e-9035" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\ln(x + y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z= x + y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z'(x) = f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x + y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\ln z+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z) = \\ln z + 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166631 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "d3145755-b414-57e5-8039-04ceb1a3a5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9037" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x + y.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = z+1.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{z+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{z+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|z+1| & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\ln{|x+y+1|}  = x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y) = \\ln{|x+y+1|}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b50ecc4f-1b95-5bad-a7ab-7236fffbc836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{z + 5} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163377} {:answer/id #uuid "da9cf03a-ffe5-5cef-b628-0ca848c3e460" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{z} - 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163378} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5d8406-deaf-5d76-aa5b-c05f83c10052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{z} + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163379} {:answer/id #uuid "152e1265-ac4b-5f2d-9bbb-022edcfff398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - 5e^{z + 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163380} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9c4bb9-e45a-5c15-be44-36b32a383fa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - e^{z + 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b50ecc4f-1b95-5bad-a7ab-7236fffbc836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{z + 5} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163377} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7af5785-1318-5b3e-94a2-5a0249e13841" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5d4c4f2-83ab-5c6a-b2e1-62527a7b057c" :question/math-academy-id "q-107797" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+2y+5}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{z+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{z+5}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2{e}^{z+5}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eea73c09-363d-57f9-8c9f-2562eab21266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{y + 3x + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163383} {:answer/id #uuid "f57169c5-806c-514b-8136-abb7e1f93a15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{y + 3x + 2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163384} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8edcf4-93dd-5d92-8a94-a8c08b36a2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{y + 3x + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163385} {:answer/id #uuid "2399e673-d222-5bf6-b2d3-9da89ae906b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{y + 3x + 2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163386} {:answer/id #uuid "5979943d-5849-5c26-928e-c3208db1cf4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{y + 3x + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8edcf4-93dd-5d92-8a94-a8c08b36a2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{y + 3x + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163385} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9c1201b-ea79-5111-a44b-c9cf706e967e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3dd7c3d-2958-59a2-969e-34c4f6fbd85e" :question/math-academy-id "q-107814" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{y+3x+2}-3.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=y+3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3 & =\\sqrt{z+2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{z+2}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z+2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (z+2)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2(z+2)^{1/2} & =x+C \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z+2} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{y+3x+2} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=2\\sqrt{y+3x+2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a0f214-a82f-5ad4-90a7-51deda39e430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡(2z+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330132} {:answer/id #uuid "285cd11a-8f32-5914-8bd4-901e35ae2c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{cos}^{2}⁡(2z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330133} {:answer/id #uuid "0fb9646e-3136-5d4a-8060-63be7ea26403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330134} {:answer/id #uuid "ef32ba9f-bd30-54e4-b321-0263fc7e08eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{cos}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330135} {:answer/id #uuid "38971647-8caf-5c5b-a7d8-2045d02ffa26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fb9646e-3136-5d4a-8060-63be7ea26403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330134} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f66041-f44e-5006-9cb0-51f8c7140a45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cadc7d0-f55e-571e-8683-ef2ab01dbbf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-108800" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡(2x-y+1).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=2x-y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={cos}^{2}⁡(z+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2-{cos}^{2}⁡(z+1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "611d910a-f061-5d9b-8e02-dfab64887b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330139} {:answer/id #uuid "869ccc6c-69d1-5f0b-8761-4d264cf47322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330140} {:answer/id #uuid "c7030cb8-72e5-5adb-992a-af6fe5d08de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-{tan}^{2}⁡(z-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330141} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9cd66f-e4bb-5662-abb7-6ace6be09272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-3{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330142} {:answer/id #uuid "8d336402-c00c-53ea-a816-7ea4fd82377b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+5\\operatorname{tan}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "869ccc6c-69d1-5f0b-8761-4d264cf47322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330140} :answer-field/id #uuid "79e07e1d-12ba-5797-b106-4412c6ccd42a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "769fb03f-07ef-54c0-8e28-ab259b3c1d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-108802" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}⁡(3x-5y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=3x-5y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-5y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={tan}^{2}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e660202-5d83-572f-8d4c-d92fee11baa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(z+2)^{5}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330146} {:answer/id #uuid "a04aa864-9f87-5b4a-82db-5615de9b9bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(z+2)^{5}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330147} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ea971f-84b0-52ee-a061-52b676f3e201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z+2)^{5}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330148} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a72c85-fa92-5882-90ad-4f9e7ac46d1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z-2)^{5}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330149} {:answer/id #uuid "937f797c-f9e6-5347-b97e-1082ac3b700a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(z+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e660202-5d83-572f-8d4c-d92fee11baa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(z+2)^{5}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330146} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff2f6a15-42e3-5a01-a856-cd34e3fe1fab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53bd63e0-b7c8-588a-b02f-e455261c85c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-108806" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(5y-7x+2)^{5}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=5y-7x,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=5y-7x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{7}{5} & =(z+2)^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5(z+2)^{5}-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=5(z+2)^{5}-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcc3e64b-ca52-54b7-bfdb-e1992800a84f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330153} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfbcec4-811a-5a3b-bc07-00be21eb9ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330154} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9c8884-3f5d-52f0-9cf4-7bea005112a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{e}^{2}}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330155} {:answer/id #uuid "1faa357a-6ac2-53aa-a0b8-96edb9191b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330156} {:answer/id #uuid "38ba1b00-7ff6-5edf-84ac-157cd5f4c444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdfbcec4-811a-5a3b-bc07-00be21eb9ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330154} :answer-field/id #uuid "2880ddbf-996b-5b4d-b960-2ba3c5b73276" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8b5df87-edf7-5da6-a482-ae2863c92ba3" :question/math-academy-id "q-108821" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{(x+y)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048b5deb-13d0-5721-b07f-ff26355736dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330160} {:answer/id #uuid "edaea162-9751-5441-93a6-f8eab2a98b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1}{x-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330161} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd07fa4-645f-5ad9-8d88-af788fbf21df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330162} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf8b0f2-1fe1-5f9e-856e-a506df1d9d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)-2}{x-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330163} {:answer/id #uuid "447036c9-9619-5da1-af16-961df9abdaea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)}{x-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edaea162-9751-5441-93a6-f8eab2a98b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1}{x-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330161} :answer-field/id #uuid "704ada25-3bfc-54cd-8f9e-e98a1a38a966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec7f57b2-2ef2-56bc-99a6-43d73009fa00" :question/math-academy-id "q-108855" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{(x-2y)^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=C-x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}} & \\,⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{z}+\\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{z}-\\frac{1}{z} & =x+C \\\\ -\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1}{z} & =x+K \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z+1}{z} & =C-x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1}{x-2y}=C-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+1}{x-2y}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7ecfb8f-7928-5f4c-a51a-914dddb78405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330167} {:answer/id #uuid "6be975f6-ee24-57cc-8589-ed289767e760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330168} {:answer/id #uuid "028cc4a9-9ebd-5997-8a43-b5ec7985c28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330169} {:answer/id #uuid "482e5744-893e-5031-b3c6-9b32ce3c882f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330170} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2812d5-d1c1-521b-8111-af3fb4a61791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2}{\\sqrt{x+y}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6be975f6-ee24-57cc-8589-ed289767e760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330168} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0cc3624-9c91-5273-9673-260ede7d1559" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f2db63f-c6f6-53bb-b50f-fb394fae795a" :question/math-academy-id "q-108856" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{x+y}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}-1.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{x}{2}+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}} & \\,⟹\\,v=2\\sqrt{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-2\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-4\\sqrt{z} & =x+K \\\\ 2\\sqrt{z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-2) & =x+K \\\\ \\sqrt{z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z-2) & =\\frac{x}{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{K}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+y}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2) & =\\frac{x}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb5b46df-4b82-5e94-99bf-e0d7213f7907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330174} {:answer/id #uuid "747df9ae-d37d-5857-89ae-e829fcbde98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330175} {:answer/id #uuid "f813ecaf-0a39-56ff-a3ef-950583546797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330176} {:answer/id #uuid "2b2ffbd7-b678-5004-a919-61ade09e054d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{2x+y}}{2x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330177} {:answer/id #uuid "718cd833-caf8-5b9b-8218-02131aad92a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f813ecaf-0a39-56ff-a3ef-950583546797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330176} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d8050a1-bba8-57c7-a54e-0ea1deee6644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86b98f38-121b-564d-b2ca-dbad04433775" :question/math-academy-id "q-108858" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{(2x+y)\\,{2}^{2x+y}}-2.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{z\\,{2}^{z}}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={2}^{z} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z\\,{2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z\\,{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+C \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x+K \\\\ {2}^{z}(z-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=K\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={2}^{2x+y}(2x+y-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee87797c-f636-5cdf-b2dc-da8ae75b2ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330181} {:answer/id #uuid "9e081cb1-bc06-564a-bd68-73f488b1a2af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330182} {:answer/id #uuid "394b669e-28a7-5ceb-a0a1-895ef0c4e9da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330183} {:answer/id #uuid "2890a8da-e979-5809-a868-1392bdce9f26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{y-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330184} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd34ff0-33ef-511a-bf9b-7dbc7bc1303f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e081cb1-bc06-564a-bd68-73f488b1a2af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330182} :answer-field/id #uuid "97c3f21d-3d69-5152-b1af-5a1b985b60b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d67c5ca6-2de3-51e3-818f-bb3a4104e2f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-149721" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-{e}^{y-3x}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=y-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3 & =3-{e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int {e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{-z} & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={e}^{3x-y}.$"}]}
